
{% extends "@templates/page.html.twig" %}

{# Set the page title. #}
{% set title = "Getting Started" %}

{# The menu title that will be imported into the header pattern. #}
{% macro menu_title() %}Getting Started{% endmacro %}

{# The page content. #}
{% block content %}

  {# Add a section to the page. #}
  {% embed "@components/sections/section--text.html.twig" with { 'heading': "Installing Kalastatic" } %}
    {% block text %}
      <p>
        Once Kalastatic is published, it is easy to include it into your project
        as a node module:
      </p>
      <figure>
        <code>
npm require kalastatic
        </code>
      </figure>
      <p>
        However, if you are looking for some demo content to help get you
        started, you can use the npm create command:
      </p>
      <figure>
        <code>
npm create kalastatic
        </code>
      </figure>
      <p>
        When running the create command, you will be prompted with questions
        about which types of demo content that you'd like to start with,
        including pages, components, styleguide, and drupal theme integration.
      </p>
    {% endblock text %}
  {% endembed %}

  {# Add a section to the page. #}
  {% embed "@components/sections/section--text.html.twig" with { 'heading': "Configuring Kalastatic" } %}
    {% block text %}
      <p>
        Since Kalastatic is a very simple system, its configuration is also
        very simple. Simply create a <code>kalastatic</code> key in your
        <code>package.json</code> to store the settings:
      </p>
      <figure>
        <code>
"kalastatic": {
}
        </code>
      </figure>


      <h3>sources</h3>
      <p>
        The first configuration item is the <code>sources</code> item, which
        contains an array of directories that Kalastatic should look in for
        <code>html.twig</code> files to process, along with the destination that
        the resulting <code>html</code> files should be placed in:
      </p>
      <figure>
        <code>
"kalastatic": {
  <strong>"sources": {
    "pages": "build"
  }</strong>
}
        </code>
      </figure>

      <h3>namespaces</h3>
      <p>
        The second configuration item is the <code>namespaces</code> item, which
        contains an array of directories that should be used for twig
        namespaces, with each item mapping a namespace name to a corresponding
        directory:
      </p>
      <figure>
        <code>
"kalastatic": {
  "sources": {
    "pages": "build"
  },
  <strong>"namespaces": {
    "components": "components",
    "templates": "templates",
    "pages": "pages"
  }</strong>
}
        </code>
      </figure>
      <p>
        Having these namespaces will allow for things like pages extending
        templates in the <code>@templates/</code> namespace and embedding
        components using the <code>@components/</code> namespace. In addition,
        Kalastatic will generate a list of files in each namespace, and pass
        the list to the twig files in a <code>namespaceFiles</code> variable.
        See the <strong>Namespace Files</strong> section below for more info.
      </p>
      <p>
        These namespaces are just common ones that we tend to use, but they
        can be named and organized in whatever way that makes sense for your
        project, along with adding additional items. For example, if you are
        buiding a styleguide, there may also be a <code>@styles/</code>
        namespace to contain sample twig files with example code and a
        <code>@styleguide/</code> namespace to contain styleguide-specific
        templates.
      </p>

      <h3>assets</h3>
      <p>
        If you have files that you would like to deploy without any processing
        needed to be done, such as image files, you can add a
        <code>assets</code> configuration item with an array of destination and
        source directories or individual files, and the files will be simply
        copied from one to the other during the build process.
      </p>
      <figure>
        <code>
"kalastatic": {
  "sources": {
    "pages": "build"
  },
  "namespaces": {
    "components": "components",
    "templates": "templates",
    "pages": "pages"
  },<strong>
  "assets": {
    "assets": "build/assets"
  }</strong>
}
        </code>
      </figure>

      <h3>styles</h3>
      <p>
        If you have scss files that need to be processed into css files, you
        can include a <code>styles</code> array with individual source and
        destination filenames (wildcard patterns have not actually been tested
        yet), and Kalatatic will use the node <code>sass</code> library to
        process those files:
      </p>
      <figure>
        <code>
"kalastatic": {
  "sources": {
    "pages": "build"
  },
  "namespaces": {
    "components": "components",
    "templates": "templates",
    "pages": "pages"
  },
  "assets": {
    "assets": "build/assets"
  },
  <strong>"styles": {
    "styles/styles.scss": "build/styles.css"
  }</strong>
}
        </code>
      </figure>
      <p>
        Note that beyond the files to compile, there is no configuration
        available for changing how the scss is processed, so if you have a more
        complicated compilation process, you can just leave out this
        configuration item and set up your own process with the compilier and
        settings of your choice.
      </p>
    {% endblock text %}
  {% endembed %}


  {# Add a section to the page. #}
  {% embed "@components/sections/section--text.html.twig" with { 'heading': "Namespace files" } %}
    {% block text %}
      <p>
        While Twig contains many helpful files for inclding content from
        other files, such as
        <a href="https://twig.symfony.com/doc/3.x/tags/embed.html">embed</a>
        and <a href="https://twig.symfony.com/doc/3.x/functions/source.html">source</a>,
        one limitation is that Twig doesn't know what files exist without
        explicity declaring them in the code.
      </p>
      <p>
        In order for Kalastatic to create things like styleguide pages that are
        dynamically generated based on whatever files are present, Kalastatic
        provides a <code>namespaceFiles</code> variable to Twig templates when
        processing them, so that the templates can iterate over the available
        files and take whatever action is needed.
      </p>
      <p>This is the general structure of the data that is passed inside of the
        <code>namespaceFiles</code> variable:
      </p>
      <figure>
        <code>
{
  "components": {
    "@components/sections/section--text.html.twig",
    "@components/sections/section--text.sample.twig",
    "@components/sections/section--text.scss",
    "@components/sections/section.html.twig",
    "@components/sections/section.sample.twig",
    "@components/sections/section.scss"
  },
  "templates": {
    "@templates/html.html.twig",
    "@templates/page.html.twig",
    "@templates/page.scss"
  }
}

        </code>
        <figcaption>
          Example values of the <code>namespaceFiles</code> variable which
          contains an array key for each namespace, along with all of the files
          contained within.
        </figcaption
      </figure>
      <p>
        For reference, here is the code that the Kalastatic demo styleguide uses
        to look for <code>.sample.twig</code> files to include in the styleguide:
      <figure>
        <code>{% verbatim %}
{# Search through the files in a namespace to create a section for each sample.twig file. #}
<strong>{% macro findSamples(namespace) %}</strong>

  {# Iterate through all of the component files and output the samples on in the styleguide. #}
  <strong>{% set sampleRoots = [] %}
  {% for file in namespaceFiles[namespace] %}
    {% if '.sample.twig' in file %}
      {% embed file with {'namespace': namespace, 'file_root': file|replace({'.sample.twig': ''}) } %}{% endembed %}
    {% endif %}
  {% endfor %}

{% endmacro %}</strong>
        {% endverbatim %}</code>
        <figcaption>
          A twig macro that accepts a namespace name, looks through the
          nested directories for sample twig files, and then embeds them on the
          current page.
        </figcaption>
      </figure>
      <p>
        Note that the filenames within the <code>namespaceFiles</code> arrays
        start with the namespaces themselves, and are ready to be plugged
        directly into Twig tags and functions without any processing.
    {% endblock text %}
  {% endembed %}



{% endblock content %}
